<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!--
        Cashier and barista.

        Two roles, not one: a small cafe has people who do both, and the owner
        asked for a user to be able to hold both at once. They are separate
        groups rather than a single "staff" group so the roles can be told apart
        later (reporting, per-role settings). Holding both is simply holding
        both; nothing here makes them exclusive.

        `group_kiosk_only` is what the two roles have in common. It is the marker
        the register guard reads (kiosk_guard.js), and it is implied by each role
        rather than granted by hand, so a user cannot end up a cashier without
        it, and a user who is both does not get it twice.
    -->

    <record id="group_kiosk_only" model="res.groups">
        <field name="name">Kiosk Only</field>
        <field name="comment">Members may only use the Kiosk register: signing in takes them straight there, and the Odoo backend sends them back to it. Implied by Cashier and by Barista, so it is not granted by hand.</field>
    </record>

    <record id="group_cashier" model="res.groups">
        <field name="name">Cashier</field>
        <field name="privilege_id" ref="point_of_sale.res_groups_privilege_point_of_sale"/>
        <field name="comment">Runs the register: takes orders and payment. Kiosk only.</field>
        <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [
            ref('base.group_user'),
            ref('group_kiosk_only'),
        ])]"/>
    </record>

    <record id="group_barista" model="res.groups">
        <field name="name">Barista</field>
        <field name="privilege_id" ref="point_of_sale.res_groups_privilege_point_of_sale"/>
        <field name="comment">Makes the orders. Kiosk only, so they can see the queue at the counter. Can be held together with Cashier.</field>
        <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [
            ref('base.group_user'),
            ref('group_kiosk_only'),
        ])]"/>
    </record>

    <!--
        Owner: the shop owner, who is not an operator.

        Deliberately NOT kiosk-only, and deliberately not implying Cashier or
        Barista — the owner is above both, not one of them. What it adds is
        sight: implying hr's Officer group is what lets a non-admin owner open
        Employees and see the team and its structure. The admin already sees
        everything, so this group exists for the other audience the owner named.
    -->
    <record id="group_owner" model="res.groups">
        <field name="name">Owner</field>
        <field name="privilege_id" ref="hr.res_groups_privilege_employees"/>
        <field name="comment">Owns the shop. Sees the team and its structure in the Employees app. Not kiosk-only: the owner is not an operator.</field>
        <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('hr.group_hr_user')])]"/>
    </record>

    <!--
        Why the two groups imply what they do, and neither implies the other.

        group_user is required: /pos/ui is a backend route, so a member without
        it cannot load the register at all.

        point_of_sale.group_pos_user is deliberately NOT implied. That group is
        what makes an account a Point of Sale user, and it carries the POS backend
        menus — Point of Sale, Orders, Sessions, Payments, Payment Methods — so
        granting it is what would put the backend within reach, guard or no guard.
        The register's access rights are instead the explicit set in ir.model.access.csv,
        granted to group_kiosk_only: the same models the till actually touches,
        owned by this module rather than borrowed from core, so the boundary can
        be tightened here and core additions to POS User do not silently land on
        cashiers and baristas.

        That is what makes kiosk-only structural rather than a UI habit: a member
        has no POS menu because no group of theirs grants one, and no rights
        outside ir.model.access.csv. Coming the other way, models/ir_http.py refuses to
        build the backend at all for a member: the shell (\u201c/odoo\u201d and \u201c/web\u201d) redirects
        them to the register, so there is no backend page for the rights above to
        guard in the first place.

        One honest limit of the mechanism: base.group_user is the floor, and it
        already grants read on a wide band of models (product.supplierinfo among
        them, verified: base.group_user grants that read on its own). Omitting an
        ACL row cannot take back what group_user grants; it can only decline to
        add more. The rows below are therefore the till's additions on top of that
        floor, and everything they name is something the floor does not already
        give.

        The kiosk rows below are this module's, and they stay here: the Access
        Matrix (kula_access_matrix) is a screen over the same ground: every
        installed menu in a row, a role in every column, and in every cell the
        degree that role has, these rows included, which it shows as already
        granted and cannot take back. It writes access rows of its own, marks
        them (kula_managed), and touches nothing else.
    -->

</odoo>
